+## C++20 Module
+
+* `module/magic_enum.cppm` exports magic_enum's own names. It does not make the standard library's operators reachable, because the standard headers it consumes are in its global module fragment and entities there are not exported. A translation unit that only does `import magic_enum;` therefore gets the types magic_enum returns without the free operators those types rely on, and code that looks correct fails to compile:
+
+  ```cpp
+  import magic_enum;
+  enum class Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };
+
+  // error: no match for 'operator!=' (operand types are
+  // 'std::basic_string_view<char>' and 'const char [5]')
+  bool blue = magic_enum::enum_name(Color::BLUE) != "BLUE";
+  ```
+
+* Include the standard headers for the types you use before the import - `<optional>`, `<string>` and `<string_view>` cover the whole API. This is not compiler-specific: it reproduces on GCC 15.2 and Clang 17. Dropping either `<optional>` or `<string>` from a consumer that compares an `enum_fuse` result and an `enum_flags_name` result breaks that consumer's build on GCC 15.2, one error each.
+
+* Put the includes before the `import`, not after: GCC reports redefinitions across the module's global module fragment when a standard header is included after it.
+
+* `module/magic_enum.cppm` builds only under a generator with C++20 module support. CMake refuses it under Unix Makefiles, so configure with `-G Ninja` and `-DMAGIC_ENUM_OPT_BUILD_MODULE=ON`, which needs CMake 3.28 or newer.
+
