+## Configuration macros
+
+Define these before including any magic_enum header, or in the header named by `MAGIC_ENUM_CONFIG_FILE`. None of them is defined by default.
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_ENABLE_HASH` - resolves `enum_cast`, `enum_index` and `enum_contains` for sparse and flags enums through a compile-time switch built from a hash of the reflected values, instead of a linear scan, and also selects that implementation for `enum_switch`. Results are unchanged; only the generated code differs.
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_ENABLE_HASH_SWITCH` - selects the same compile-time switch for `enum_switch` alone, leaving `enum_cast`, `enum_index` and `enum_contains` on the linear scan. Defining `MAGIC_ENUM_ENABLE_HASH` already implies it.
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_NO_ASSERT` - makes magic_enum's internal assertion macro expand to nothing. By default it expands to `assert` from `<cassert>`, so a violated precondition aborts in a build with assertions live; with this macro the call returns the function's fallback value instead. Defining `MAGIC_ENUM_ASSERT` yourself routes the same checks to your own handler.
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_NO_CHECK_REFLECTED_ENUM` - skips the check that an enum has at least one value inside the reflected range. Without it, such an enum fails to compile with "magic_enum requires enum implementation and valid max and min"; with it the code compiles and the functions return empty results.
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_NO_CHECK_SUPPORT` - compiles on a compiler magic_enum does not support instead of failing with a compilation error. See the Synopsis above and [limitations](limitations.md).
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_NO_EXCEPTION` - makes the container error paths call `std::abort()` rather than throw. By default, and when the compiler has exceptions enabled, `containers::bitset` and `containers::set` throw `std::out_of_range` and `std::invalid_argument`; the macro is implied when the compiler has exceptions disabled.
+
+* `MAGIC_ENUM_USE_STD_MODULE` - stops the headers from including the standard headers they use, so a translation unit that has done `import std;` supplies them instead. `module/magic_enum.cppm` uses it for that configuration; see the C++20 Module section of [limitations](limitations.md).
+
+The remaining configuration macros are documented where the feature they affect is: `MAGIC_ENUM_RANGE_MIN` and `MAGIC_ENUM_RANGE_MAX` under [`customize::enum_range`](#customizeenum_range), `MAGIC_ENUM_USING_ALIAS_OPTIONAL`, `MAGIC_ENUM_USING_ALIAS_STRING`, `MAGIC_ENUM_USING_ALIAS_STRING_VIEW` and `MAGIC_ENUM_CONFIG_FILE` in the Synopsis above, `MAGIC_ENUM_NO_TYPESAFE_ENUM_FUSE` under [`enum_fuse`](#enum_fuse), and `MAGIC_ENUM_SUPPORTED` in the Synopsis and in [limitations](limitations.md).
